Description: antinomie: publish the identity of the files a call acted on, and the image an exec loaded
Author: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Origin: project
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-21

A path is a name; the same file has several (renames, hard links) and the same name can mean different files over time. Two optional records let the host tell. lifecycle.file_id (antinomie.fileid/1, 32 bytes) carries the device, inode, generation, link count and mode of the file an open returned, a rename moved, a link named or an unlink removed (with the links that remain), in the traced call itself. lifecycle.exec_image (antinomie.execimg/1) is published once per exec at the commit point: the resolved path and identity of the image that was loaded and, for a script, the interpreter exactly as its #! line names it (bprm->interp), which the hash record could only flag.  io_uring requests carry the same identity as the operand async.operand.file_id. OPENAT and OPENAT2 attach the file they opened; RENAMEAT, LINKAT and UNLINKAT run on a worker with no traced call of their own, so the name is looked up just before the operation; a request that acts on an open regular file (read, write, fsync, ftruncate, fallocate, sync_file_range, fadvise) attaches the file it used, once, so a descriptor of a registered table is tied to its file. An AIO request on a regular file does the same.  Nothing changes for a host that does not know the records, and a host that does falls back to names when a guest does not publish them. The contract revision stays 17.

